-- title:
--   In an association, right orbits are principal right ideals
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ be an **association**: every element is associating. For $a\in A$ let $aA=\{c:\exists b,\ a\cdot b=c\}$ be its right orbit.
--
--   $$aA\ \text{is a right ideal, for every } a\in A.$$
--
--   An ideal of this shape is **principal**, generated by $a$. The manuscript observes that $aA$ is a right ideal precisely when every product $(ab)c$ can be rewritten as $ad$ for some $d$, and that in an association one may take $d=bc$ — the associating hypothesis on the middle factor is exactly what produces $bc$ and the required equality.
-- source:
--   Andrew Winkler, *Ideals in Balanced Algebras and the Genesis of Mathematics*, manuscript dated 20 March 2020, §4.1 (p. 6): "Consider now the right orbit aA. If an ideal is of this form, for some a, it is called principal, generated by a. ... Thus in every association, right orbits are right (principal) ideals."

import Definitions.Def_BalancedAlgebra_core

namespace BalancedAlgebra

open PartialAlgebra

theorem rightOrbit_rightIdeal {A : Type*} (P : PartialAlgebra A) (hP : P.IsAssociation) (a : A) :
    P.RightIdeal (P.rightOrbit a) := by sorry

end BalancedAlgebra
